<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Lifelong asthma in a 23-year-old athlete, uncontrolled on albuterol alone. Please confirm the diagnosis and severity, assess for type 2 inflammation and allergic triggers, and advise on treatment step and exercise. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Routine, within four weeks. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Do you agree that this is moderate persistent asthma, and is anything else, such as vocal cord dysfunction, contributing?</item><item>Is there evidence of type 2 or allergic inflammation, and which allergen measures are worthwhile?</item><item>Is Step 4 maintenance-and-reliever therapy the right level, and when should we step down?</item><item>How should he manage symptoms during boxing and rowing?</item><item>Is he a candidate for allergen immunotherapy or a biologic now or later?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Roosevelt is seen at the request of Dr. Kettering four weeks after starting budesonide-formoterol maintenance-and-reliever therapy. He describes asthma since early childhood, quiet for some years in his teens and worse again over the past winter with colds. Since starting the new inhaler his symptoms have fallen to two or three days a week and night waking to once in four weeks. Colds and dusty rooms are his triggers. He has no nasal symptoms, reflux, snoring, stridor, or episodes of sudden throat tightness with normal breathing between. He believes that hard training is what keeps his asthma in check. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Theodore Roosevelt was born at 28 East 20th Street, New York City, on October 27, 1858. [D]</item><item>In his autobiography Roosevelt wrote that he was a sickly, delicate boy who suffered much from asthma and frequently had to be taken away on trips to find a place where he could breathe; he remembered sitting up in bed gasping at night as a very small child. [D]</item><item>When he was nearly fourteen, during an attack of asthma, he was sent alone to Moosehead Lake; after two other boys on the stage-coach easily overpowered him, he began boxing lessons and a long program of physical training, with his father&apos;s approval. [D]</item><item>Roosevelt wrote that he was very near-sighted as a boy without knowing it, and that at about thirteen, after he could not read a distant billboard, he got his first pair of spectacles. [D]</item><item>A 2015 review of the historical record in the Journal of Allergy and Clinical Immunology: In Practice concluded that his respiratory problems began in early childhood, that the record gives strong evidence for poorly controlled persistent asthma, that the asthma became relatively quiet in adolescence when his exercise program began at about 12, and that he continued to have serious exacerbations in adolescence and adulthood; it found little if any support for the claim that his asthma was psychosomatic. [R]</item><item>Roosevelt received his Bachelor of Arts degree from Harvard in June 1880 and was elected to the New York State Assembly in 1881. [D]</item><item>His father, Theodore Roosevelt Sr., died in 1878 shortly after being diagnosed with stomach cancer. [D]</item></list><paragraph>2 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

