<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Moderate persistent asthma (J45.40). Confirmed: variable symptoms with reversible obstruction (17 percent and 540 mL bronchodilator response). Severity before treatment was moderate persistent by NAEPP impairment criteria (FEV1 72 percent predicted, night waking about twice a week). It is improving on Step 4 therapy, with an Asthma Control Test of 18. Nothing suggests vocal cord dysfunction. [S]</item><item>Type 2 inflammation and allergic sensitization. FeNO 46 ppb on inhaled corticosteroid, blood eosinophils 0.42 x10³/µL, and total IgE 260 IU/mL indicate type 2 inflammation. House dust mite specific IgE 8.4 kU/L shows sensitization that fits his symptoms in dusty rooms. [S]</item><item>Exercise-related symptoms. Mostly a sign of incomplete control, not a separate disorder. Exercise is good for him but does not treat the airway inflammation. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Intake spirometry with bronchodilator response, the Asthma Control Test scores, and today&apos;s FeNO, blood eosinophil count, total IgE, and house dust mite specific IgE. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Continue budesonide-formoterol two inhalations twice daily and one as needed, up to 12 inhalations a day; the corrected technique and an evening-dose reminder should close most of the remaining gap. [S]</item><item>Repeat spirometry three months after starting therapy. [S]</item><item>Consider stepping down to one inhalation twice daily after at least three months of good control with no exacerbation. [S]</item><item>One reliever inhalation 5 to 20 minutes before boxing or rowing, with a proper warm-up; keep training. [S]</item><item>Dust-mite-impermeable mattress and pillow covers and weekly hot washing of bedding, since sensitization is documented. [S]</item><item>Allergen immunotherapy is not needed while asthma is controlled; reconsider if dust-mite-related symptoms persist once control is stable. A biologic is not indicated, because the asthma is not severe. [S]</item><item>PCV20 today for chronic lung disease, a yearly influenza vaccine, and a COVID-19 vaccine by CDC guidance. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Kettering the same day; the patient agrees with the plan and asked for a written summary for his training log. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Primary care will continue monitoring on the CMID schedule. Pulmonology will see him again for an exacerbation needing emergency care, two or more oral corticosteroid courses in a year while on treatment, or loss of control despite good adherence and technique. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
